Subject: Gives every register opcode a one-instruction step form

What this does
  Gives every register opcode a one-instruction step form.

Why we need it
  Generated code has to interleave its own instructions with interpreted ones and take back control after each, but the register interpreter's handlers tail-call into their successor, so there is no way to run exactly one instruction and return.

What it adds
  `regvm_step` dispatches through a per-opcode `regvm_step_table`: one function per opcode that runs a single instruction and returns the next `ip`. A generator extracts each opcode's monolith body and rewrites frame references to the run passed in; hand-written forms cover the cases that are not one instruction (return, yield/yield_star, await, initial yield, the unimplemented-opcode trap). `QJS_REGVM_STEPCHECK` routes the monolith interpreter through `regvm_step` so the step forms are exercised by the full suite; `regvm_step_uncovered` reports how many opcodes have no step form.

Needs a bytecode version bump
  No.
diff --git a/quickjs-regvm-step.inc b/quickjs-regvm-step.inc
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..0f59830
--- /dev/null
+++ b/quickjs-regvm-step.inc
@@ -0,0 +1,5958 @@
+/* Generated from the monolith's `L_ROP_*` bodies -- do not edit by hand.
+ * Included by quickjs.c after js_regvm_execute; the RD_* and SET_REG_* macros
+ * are still defined at that point. */
+
+#undef  REGVM_NEXT
+#define REGVM_NEXT() return rpc
+
+static uint8_t *REGVM_CC regvm_s_ROP_add(uint8_t *ip, JSValue *fr, JSRegRun *rf, JSContext *ctx)
+{
+    JSFunctionBytecode *b = rf->b;
+    JSObject *p = JS_VALUE_GET_OBJ(rf->sf->cur_func);
+    JSRegPIC *pic = rf->pic;
+    uint8_t *rpc = ip;
+    int op = *rpc++;
+    JSValue ret_val = JS_UNDEFINED;
+    JSValue *regs = fr;
+    (void)b; (void)p; (void)pic; (void)rpc; (void)op; (void)ret_val; (void)regs;
+ { REGVM_BIN_OP(REG_BIN_ADD); }
+ exception:
+    rf->ret = JS_EXCEPTION;
+    return NULL;
+}
